{
  "text": " in terms of publishing, in terms of publishing, generally speaking, there's been a hyper surveillance and a, and really a policing of thought around what they think, what they want to write about, how they convey lived experience through very restrictive normative binaries that often are situated in whiteness. If we're being honest, then we're going to have a grown up conversation about this. So I think the idea that this kind of pushes those boundaries, I think is really important. And it's giving people, you know, one of the most important things I think anyone can have, which is this sense of agency and ability to express yourself in the truest sense of the word, and in a very autonomous and cathartic and empowering way and an emancipatory way as well. That's such a brilliant and beautiful answer, Jason, as always. I think that one of the things that you've talked about there is, yeah, that importance of agency, that importance of lived experience, and really taking seriously how like marginalization, yes, it is such a difficult thing to navigate within education and institutions more broadly, but actually it means we have a unique, I'm going to use one of your words, unique vista into understanding society. And that's why it's just so incredible that Sage are supporting this in a meaningful way. Yeah, Sage are supporting in a meaningful way. And I've often said this, and I mean it when I say it, I've been very fortunate to have met the most remarkable black women in my just over a decade in academia. You know, CJ, you know, I'll often say you're probably the best person I've met in academia. And but someone that's a very close second to that, you know, is Delaina Spencer. And I often credit her single handedly with ensuring that this doesn't become another generation of lost academics in terms of black and ethnic minority people not getting the opportunity to tell their stories. I think what Delaina has done over the last six, seven years in terms of developing mechanisms and interventions for black academics across the career lifeline, early career, mid career, you know, very senior, is that she's kind of given this platform for them to talk about their experiences, legitimize those experiences and has used substantive resource that Sage have in addition to her own ingenuity and brilliance to really drive and motivate this thing. And she's really been at the vanguard of that change and the c-section of change we've observed in the last five years in terms of publishing, I genuinely would single handedly put it down to one Delaina Spencer. Come on Delaina Spencer, also on this episode as well. But yeah, you're so brilliant at giving black women, especially their flowers, Jason. So on that note, how does academic scholarship create impact beyond the academy? CJ, that's a brilliant question. Do you know what, it's kind of, it's kind of the impossible dream. It's one of the things that I've always been, I've been so gravitated towards your scholarship because you're one of the few people I've observed who's managed to do that, you know, to take academia, to make accessible, to find ways to make it more inclusive, more engaging. I think one of the biggest things is how we kind of accessibility. Yeah. I think we can engage in this kind of linguistic Olympics where we just make things completely inaccessible. I think we need to understand there are different modes of learning, different modes of engagement, different modes of knowledge exchange and consumption. And I guess one of the things that's kind of been amazing about a conduit like surviving society is that it's managed to hold court in terms of being front facing and kind of illuminating academia and what happens in academia, but it's also showed that idea of real life. And so I often talk about practice, you know, and this idea we theorize about ideas, but what does it mean practically speaking? And it's one, it's something that's a euphemism that's located in the classroom space. But if we kind of think about, you know, the classroom of life, how do we take these ideas from these kind of archaic, problematic ivory towers and put them in the real world and make them tangible, you know, something people can actually touch. And I think for me, something like surviving society has done that well. It's done exceptionally well. And what it's become is it's been the stimulus for other people to be more exploratory in the ways in which they engage in public sociology and other public discourses and other public academic disciplines, you know, which can range from STEM to sociology, to psychology, to education, to life sciences. I think that's important because what it's given us is a new way of thinking and over the last five years, there may be some people who are coming up through the kind of pipeline now who this will be the norm for them. And that's great. But what they won't realise is that before the intervention of surviving society, that wasn't. And actually that intervention ended up being the catalyst for a lot of different thinking about the ways we can engage in publishing and knowledge dissemination. And that's a very key thing. And I think, you know, smartly, Sage caught the gaitaist of that. And I wouldn't say jumped on the bandwagon, but they knew where to hitch their ride. And for me, they hitched it on the correct way.",
